Feature: Public endpoint probes
  A blackbox exporter that the tree runs in the namespace monitoring probes every route of the public Gateway over HTTPS and the redirect over plain HTTP, from inside the cluster, through the Gateway's proxies by the Service's cluster name, with the hostname given for TLS and Host. An application declares its probe beside its route, the cluster's overlay selects the exporter and the probes, and the alerts on them are in features/alerts.feature.

  Scenario: A route on the public Gateway is probed over HTTPS through the Gateway, by name, without leaving the cluster
    Given the render of the tree, each application as Flux builds it offline with the example cluster-config and each release as its chart renders it, the demo route, the public Gateway application and the exporter among them, a copy of the route and the Gateway rendered with another domain, the Gateway taken into another namespace than the controller's, and the controller configured to deploy the proxies in each Gateway's namespace or without its configuration
    When the Probe of the route, the Probe of the redirect, the Probe of the route behind the cluster's sign-in, the targets of every Probe over HTTPS, the Gateway's proxies' Service name and namespace, the controller's version and deployment mode, the exporter's Service and its modules, and the alerts' rules are read
    Then each Probe's one target is the proxies' Service by its cluster name, derived by the rule read in the controller's source at the version the render runs, in the controller's namespace whichever namespace the Gateway is in, the controller configured to deploy the proxies in each Gateway's namespace, or without its configuration, being refused, with the Gateway's address in no literal and the route's hostname in no target, and the hostname, which follows the cluster's domain, goes to the exporter as the parameter that sets Host and the name TLS checks; the route's module takes only a 200 over HTTPS, the redirect's only a 301 over plain HTTP that is not followed, the module of the route behind the cluster's sign-in only the 302 that starts a sign-in, over HTTPS and not followed, and the exporter, in the namespace monitoring, is the Probes' prober; each other Probe over HTTPS asks for a path its application answers 200 without a session; kromgo's Probe asks for a badge kromgo's configuration serves, as kromgo answers any badge path with a 200; and every series the alerts' rules read, as the render applies them, is of the job the Probes set

  Scenario: The probe verifies the certificate against the trust the cluster's issuer implies
    Given the render of the tree, whose cluster chooses letsencrypt-production for the Gateway's certificate, and copies of it whose cluster chooses nothing, which is the cluster's own CA, letsencrypt-staging, and an ACME issuer the probes hold no values for
    When the issuer the Gateway's Certificate asks and the exporter's module and pod are read from each render, and the kubernetes check is run on the copies that choose staging and the issuer without values
    Then the module that probes a route behind sign-in verifies as the other module over HTTPS does, under every issuer; with letsencrypt-production and the cluster's own CA no module skips verification; with letsencrypt-production the module that probes over HTTPS verifies against the system's roots of the exporter's image, naming no CA and mounting no Secret; with the cluster's own CA it trusts that authority alone, through the one key of a Secret that holds its public certificate, never a private key; with letsencrypt-staging, whose chain no client trusts, the module skips verification, which is the one setting of its TLS configuration, is otherwise the production one, no module but the two over HTTPS skips it, the pod holds no Secret and the kubernetes check accepts the cluster; probes that trust another issuer than the Gateway's certificate comes from are refused, either way round; and the cluster that chooses an issuer the probes hold no values for is refused, the check naming the file to add, and its render fails on the key the exporter's release would not find

  Scenario: A route on the public Gateway without a probe fails the checks
    Given the routes the render of the tree attaches to the public Gateway, those Flux builds with their components and substitutions and those a chart renders from a release's values alike, a copy of the tree with a route for a hostname that no Probe names, a copy whose release's chart renders such a route from its values, and then its Probe as well, then such a route inside a List the chart renders, and then its Probe in the same List, and renders whose route behind the cluster's sign-in has no Probe, a Probe for 200 in place of or beside its Probe of the sign-in redirect, or no longer a policy with external authorisation, whose unprotected route is probed for the redirect, and whose Probe of a route holds something beyond the fields that make it a probe: a path or a scheme of the prober, an interval, a scrape timeout or metric relabelings of its own, discovered targets, a second target, a label beside the hostname, or a relabeling that writes another label than the hostname parameter
    When each route's hostnames are matched with the hostnames and modules of the Probes in the same render
    Then every route of the tree has a Probe for each hostname and for the redirect, one Probe covering every route of its hostname, the sign-in paths with the page they guard; the copy with the unprobed route fails, naming its hostname, and so does the copy whose chart renders the route, naming the route the chart makes, until the chart renders its Probe, which counts as one a manifest declares, and so does the copy whose chart renders the route inside a List, naming the item, until the List holds its Probe; a hostname behind sign-in without a Probe of the redirect fails, naming each of its routes; a Probe for 200 of a hostname behind sign-in, or of the redirect of one that is not, fails, naming the Probe; and a Probe that holds something beyond the fields that make it a probe does not count, so its route fails as one without a Probe

  Scenario: The Gateway does not require the PROXY protocol header while the probes reach its listeners from inside the cluster
    Given the render of the tree, whose Probes reach the public Gateway's listeners through the proxies' Service by its cluster name and write no PROXY protocol header, and copies of it whose Gateway's ClientTrafficPolicy requires the header, by proxyProtocol without optional, with optional false or by the deprecated enableProxyProtocol, takes no header at all, requires it on a cluster with no Probe, or requires it on a Gateway that is, with its policy, in another namespace than the controller, whose Probes reach the proxies' Service the controller makes in its own namespace
    When the Gateway's ClientTrafficPolicy is read beside the Probes' targets
    Then the tree passes, its policy taking the header as optional; each copy whose policy requires the header fails, naming the policy, the Gateway and every Probe and saying the probes would be cut, the copy whose Gateway is in another namespace through the Service in the controller's namespace; and the copy that takes no header and the one without Probes pass

  Scenario: A cluster that selects the probes without the exporter or the Gateway is refused
    Given the render of the tree, the render of a copy of it in which two applications that do not name the exporter in dependsOn hold a Probe, one among its manifests and one that its release's chart renders, and a copy of the tree whose overlay lists the exporter, the Gateway, the route and the Gateway's issuer
    When the applications of each render that hold a Probe are matched with the dependsOn of their Kustomizations, and the overlay leaves out the exporter, and then only the Gateway
    Then every application of the tree that holds a Probe names the exporter, the two that do not are refused, each by its name, and the kubernetes check refuses both overlays, naming the Kustomizations that declare a Probe, the one they need and the overlay
